<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Buton Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1332 CE – 1960 CE (approx.) · capital Wolio (Baubau)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An island sultanate off southeast Sulawesi, known for its massive Wolio fortress and an unusual elected monarchy that lasted until 1960.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buton-island">Buton</a>'s chronicles trace its kingdom to the 14th century and a legendary first queen, Wa Kaa Kaa. Placed on the sea route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gowa-sultanate">Makassar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>, it grew as a port and a power over the nearby islands. Its sixth king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakilaponto">Lakilaponto</a>, adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> around 1540 and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Murhum.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buton-island">Buton</a> was governed by a written constitution, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martabat-tujuh">Martabat Tujuh</a>, adopted under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> La Elangi in the early 17th century and inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> thought. Sultans were elected by a council of nobles from rotating royal houses and could be deposed. The Wolio fortress, with walls nearly three kilometres long, protected the capital.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Squeezed between Gowa and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buton-island">Buton</a> allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> from 1613. It kept internal autonomy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule. After the death of the last sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-falihi-kaimuddin">Muhammad Falihi Kaimuddin</a>, in 1960, the sultanate ended and its lands became regencies of Southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/buton-sultanate.jpg" alt="Buton Sultanate" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: PhantomFunds · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultanate_of_Buton_Locator.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1332 CE – 1960 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wolio (Baubau)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Murhum, La Elangi, Muhammad Falihi Kaimuddin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Sufi influenced)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Wolio</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wolio, Butung, Sultanate of Buton</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1646&amp;focus=buton-sultanate" aria-label="Buton Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8078 2382 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Buton Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8398 2638L8538 2404M8398 2638L7959 2663M8398 2638L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8538 2404h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8398 2638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8398 2638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1646&amp;focus=buton-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
